1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,690
>> Imagine a party with 23 guests.

2
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:09,210
>> What are the chances that two of them share a birthday?

3
00:00:09,290 --> 00:00:12,830
>> With 365 days to choose from, a match sounds pretty unlikely.

4
00:00:12,910 --> 00:00:16,750
>> And yet it's more likely than not: 50.7%.

5
00:00:16,830 --> 00:00:24,700
>> Better than a coin toss, with just 23 people?

6
00:00:24,780 --> 00:00:28,900
>> Yes. And with 70 guests, it's 99.9%: almost certain.

7
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:37,320
>> This is the birthday problem. Let's find out why it works.

8
00:00:42,420 --> 00:00:48,290
>> Let's set some rules for our party first.

9
00:00:48,370 --> 00:00:50,360
>> Each birthday is one of 365 days, all equally likely, and we'll leave out February 29.

10
00:00:50,440 --> 00:00:57,600
>> Now the guests arrive one at a time, and we mark each birthday on the calendar.

11
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:00,820
>> So far, every guest has a day to themselves.

12
00:01:00,900 --> 00:01:07,320
Until guest 20 lands on a day that's already taken: November 19, the birthday of guest 7.

13
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:11,620
>> Twenty guests, and we already have a match.

14
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:18,560
That's just one party, though. So here are 1,000 more, each with 23 random guests.

15
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:23,510
>> 511 of them have a shared birthday: about half, just as the maths says.

16
00:01:23,590 --> 00:01:32,260
>> That's far fewer guests than our instincts suggest. In one study, most people markedly overestimated the number needed.

17
00:01:32,340 --> 00:01:37,230
>> So who first noticed this?

18
00:01:37,310 --> 00:01:44,220
>> It's usually credited to the mathematician Harold Davenport, around 1927.

19
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:50,890
>> But he didn't publish it, or claim it as his own: he couldn't believe nobody had stated it before.

20
00:01:50,970 --> 00:01:59,520
>> The first published version came from Richard von Mises, in 1939.

21
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:05,530
>> So why does our intuition get this so wrong?

22
00:02:05,610 --> 00:02:10,500
>> It's natural to think about your own birthday, and whether anyone matches it.

23
00:02:10,580 --> 00:02:17,250
>> Right, and with 22 other guests, the chance that someone shares yours is only about 6%.

24
00:02:17,330 --> 00:02:22,620
>> Six percent? That's tiny.

25
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:29,040
>> But any two guests can make a match, not just you and someone else.

26
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:34,410
>> So let's draw a line between every pair of guests.

27
00:02:34,490 --> 00:02:40,980
>> The first guest pairs with 22 others, the next with 21 more, and so on.

28
00:02:41,060 --> 00:02:47,350
>> The lines just keep coming.

29
00:02:47,430 --> 00:02:55,270
That's 23 × 22 ÷ 2: 253 pairs.

30
00:02:55,350 --> 00:02:59,290
>> So that's 253 chances for a match, not 22.

31
00:02:59,370 --> 00:03:02,210
>> Exactly. Only 22 of those pairs include you. The other 231 are matches you weren't counting.

32
00:03:02,290 --> 00:03:06,950
>> And pairs grow fast. Double the guests to 46, and there are 1,035 pairs: about four times as many.

33
00:03:07,030 --> 00:03:16,750
>> So pairs grow like the square of the guests. That will matter later.

34
00:03:19,350 --> 00:03:24,890
For the exact chance, it's easier to work out the opposite: that no two guests share a birthday.

35
00:03:24,970 --> 00:03:32,810
>> The chance that everyone is different. Then the rest is the chance of a match.

36
00:03:32,890 --> 00:03:40,160
So, the first guest can have any birthday: 365 days out of 365.

37
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:47,680
>> The second must miss the first: 364 out of 365.

38
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:53,850
>> The third must miss both: 363 out of 365. And so on.

39
00:03:53,930 --> 00:04:00,570
>> Until guest 23, who must miss all 22 before: 343 out of 365.

40
00:04:00,650 --> 00:04:05,060
>> Multiply all 23 fractions, and the chance that every birthday differs is 49.3%.

41
00:04:05,140 --> 00:04:11,650
>> So the chance of at least one shared birthday is 50.7%.

42
00:04:11,730 --> 00:04:14,450
>> Every one of those fractions looks so harmless, though.

43
00:04:14,530 --> 00:04:18,770
>> Each one is close to 1, but there are many, and each new guest has more birthdays to avoid.

44
00:04:18,850 --> 00:04:26,370
>> Let's do the same sum for every size of party, and watch the chance climb.

45
00:04:26,450 --> 00:04:32,720
>> 10 guests: 11.7%. 30 guests: 70.6%. 50 guests: 97%.

46
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:38,490
>> It passes 99% at 57 guests, and 99.9% at 70.

47
00:04:38,570 --> 00:04:43,160
>> So when is a match actually guaranteed?

48
00:04:43,240 --> 00:04:49,540
>> Only at 366 guests, when there are more guests than days.

49
00:04:52,940 --> 00:04:57,060
Let's watch it happen. Guests enter a room one by one, and we stop at the first shared birthday.

50
00:04:57,140 --> 00:05:02,800
>> In this room, the first match came with guest 28.

51
00:05:02,880 --> 00:05:07,070
>> In this one, guest 33. And in this one, guest 18.

52
00:05:07,150 --> 00:05:14,690
>> It really does vary from room to room.

53
00:05:14,770 --> 00:05:18,970
>> So let's run a thousand rooms. Each dot is one room, placed at the guest who brought its first match.

54
00:05:19,220 --> 00:05:26,240
>> A few rooms matched very early, with just 3 guests. A few went past 60.

55
00:05:26,320 --> 00:05:31,690
>> In 511 of the 1,000 rooms, the first match came by guest 23.

56
00:05:31,770 --> 00:05:34,880
>> Just over half, then.

57
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:46,950
>> And on average, the first match took 24.7 guests. The theory says about 24.6.

58
00:05:47,030 --> 00:05:52,520
>> Real groups behave the same way. At the 2014 World Cup, each of the 32 squads had 23 players.

59
00:05:52,600 --> 00:05:57,170
>> So every squad was one of our rooms of 23.

60
00:05:57,250 --> 00:06:02,090
>> And an analysis of the squad lists suggested that 16 of them had a shared birthday: exactly half.

61
00:06:02,170 --> 00:06:06,890
>> Five of those squads even had two pairs.

62
00:06:10,490 --> 00:06:12,910
>> Now let's change the question: how many people do you need for an even chance that someone shares your birthday?

63
00:06:12,990 --> 00:06:16,200
>> Half of 365, surely? About 183?

64
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:22,120
>> Let's see. Each person matches you with a chance of 1 in 365.

65
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:24,640
>> So the chance that nobody does is 364 out of 365, multiplied once for every person.

66
00:06:24,720 --> 00:06:29,420
>> That shrinks slowly. It takes 253 people to reach an even chance.

67
00:06:29,500 --> 00:06:32,130
>> That's far more than half of 365. Where do all the extra people go?

68
00:06:32,210 --> 00:06:37,270
>> Many of them share birthdays with each other, so they don't cover any new days.

69
00:06:37,350 --> 00:06:45,380
>> Drop 253 random birthdays on the calendar, and they land on only 180 different days.

70
00:06:45,460 --> 00:06:50,550
>> So your birthday has only about an even chance of being one of them.

71
00:06:50,630 --> 00:06:54,420
>> Now look at that number again: 253. It's exactly the number of pairs among 23 guests.

72
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:58,050
>> Is that a coincidence?

73
00:06:58,130 --> 00:07:01,500
>> Not really. Both questions need about 253 chances, each of 1 in 365.

74
00:07:01,580 --> 00:07:05,890
>> A 1-in-365 chance becomes an even bet after about 253 tries: 365 × ln 2.

75
00:07:06,590 --> 00:07:09,550
>> So the room of 23 and the crowd of 253 are really the same sum.

76
00:07:12,150 --> 00:07:17,300
And that gives us a quick rule for any number of days, not just 365.

77
00:07:17,380 --> 00:07:23,710
>> With n guests, there are about n² ÷ 2 pairs.

78
00:07:23,790 --> 00:07:27,390
>> Like half of a square grid: every guest against every other guest.

79
00:07:27,470 --> 00:07:33,810
>> For an even chance we need about 253 pairs, so n² should be about 506.

80
00:07:33,890 --> 00:07:38,810
>> The square root of 506 is about 22.5. Round up, and you get 23.

81
00:07:38,890 --> 00:07:45,040
>> In general, you need about 1.18 × √days.

82
00:07:45,120 --> 00:07:49,030
>> So what about a year with a million days?

83
00:07:49,110 --> 00:07:53,700
>> With a million equally likely days, just 1,178 people would give an even chance.

84
00:07:53,780 --> 00:08:02,360
>> Out of a million days? That's about a tenth of a percent.

85
00:08:02,440 --> 00:08:06,070
>> That's the real surprise: the people needed grow only like the square root of the days.

86
00:08:08,670 --> 00:08:11,950
Of course, our party made some simplifications: 365 equally likely days, no February 29, and no twins.

87
00:08:12,030 --> 00:08:14,340
>> And real birthdays aren't spread evenly. In the US, more babies tend to be born in September and October.

88
00:08:14,420 --> 00:08:19,760
>> From 1973 to 1999, September 16 was the most common US birthday, and Christmas Day the rarest, apart from February 29.

89
00:08:19,840 --> 00:08:21,080
>> So does that break the maths? No: any unevenness only makes a shared birthday more likely.

90
00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:27,600
>> More likely? Why's that?

91
00:08:27,680 --> 00:08:33,810
>> Take an extreme case: imagine everyone was born in the first half of the year.

92
00:08:33,890 --> 00:08:39,150
>> Then just 17 guests would give a better-than-even chance.

93
00:08:39,230 --> 00:08:44,010
>> Fewer days to go round, so more people crowd onto the same ones.

94
00:08:44,090 --> 00:08:49,910
>> Real birthdays are only slightly uneven, though, so the real-world answer is still 23.

95
00:08:49,990 --> 00:08:55,070
>> And twins nearly always share a birthday, which can only raise the chance.

96
00:08:57,670 --> 00:09:01,480
The same maths matters in computer security, too.

97
00:09:01,560 --> 00:09:02,870
>> A hash function turns any file into a short digital fingerprint of fixed length.

98
00:09:02,950 --> 00:09:12,700
>> A 64-bit fingerprint has about 18 billion billion possible values.

99
00:09:12,780 --> 00:09:16,200
>> That sounds like plenty.

100
00:09:16,280 --> 00:09:22,320
>> Still, two different files with the same fingerprint make a collision.

101
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:27,850
>> And fingerprints are like birthdays, in a year with an enormous number of days.

102
00:09:27,930 --> 00:09:34,700
>> So collisions show up after about the square root of that number: 2 to the power of half the bits.

103
00:09:34,780 --> 00:09:39,700
>> For 64 bits, about 5 billion tries give an even chance of a collision.

104
00:09:39,780 --> 00:09:48,690
>> And to match one particular fingerprint?

105
00:09:48,770 --> 00:09:59,020
>> That's like matching your own birthday: it takes about 13 billion billion tries.

106
00:09:59,100 --> 00:10:01,440
>> That's the idea behind the birthday attack. Imagine a forger with a fair contract and a fraudulent one.

107
00:10:01,520 --> 00:10:04,830
>> She makes many harmless variations of each: an extra comma, a double space, a synonym.

108
00:10:04,910 --> 00:10:09,850
>> When a fair version and a fraudulent version share a fingerprint, she has her collision.

109
00:10:09,930 --> 00:10:15,390
>> Her victim signs the fair version, and the signature fits the fraudulent one just as well.

110
00:10:15,470 --> 00:10:17,190
>> That's sneaky. So how do you defend against it?

111
00:10:17,270 --> 00:10:22,040
>> With longer fingerprints: about twice as many bits as brute force alone would demand.

112
00:10:22,120 --> 00:10:28,080
>> SHA-256 has 256 bits, so a collision takes around 2 to the 128 tries: a 39-digit number.

113
00:10:28,730 --> 00:10:33,950
>> So a party puzzle tells engineers how long their fingerprints need to be.

114
00:10:36,550 --> 00:10:41,920
So, back to the party.

115
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:46,760
>> 23 guests sound like too few, because we picture matches with ourselves.

116
00:10:46,840 --> 00:10:51,760
>> But every pair is a chance, and 23 guests make 253 pairs.

117
00:10:51,840 --> 00:10:56,290
>> That's enough for a better-than-even chance: 50.7%.

118
00:10:56,370 --> 00:10:58,190
>> It still feels wrong, doesn't it? Even when you can see every pair.

119
00:10:58,270 --> 00:11:02,860
>> That's what makes it a paradox: it seems wrong at first, but it's true.

120
00:11:02,940 --> 00:11:06,610
>> So next time you're in a room of 23 people or more, ask around.

121
00:11:06,690 --> 00:11:12,180
>> More often than not, two of you will share a birthday.
